## CMK Language & Compiler
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

Most documentation doesn't emphasize that `compose.mk` is actually *a programming language*.  The shocking secret truth is safe to ignore, and you don't need to care about this if you're only interested in [tools]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/tool-mode) or a [standard-library]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/standard-lib) for Makefiles.

This section is hands-on with the language itself, and enough details to use it or to extend it.  Other pages you might be looking for instead:

* [CMK Quick Reference]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/cmk-quick-ref), a cheat-sheet of important CMK-lang syntax.
* [CMK Demo Index]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/cmk-demos): a single cross-referenced listing of every featured demo (and its plain-Makefile twin), see the .  Most CMK demos are direct ports from existing pure-Makefile demos that are twins.
* CMK demos in the source tree @ {{macros.repo_link('demos/cmk', github=github)}}
* [Language Classification/Properties]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/language#language-properties)

### Introduction 
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

{%include "cmk-quick-version.md.j2"%}
Since CMK inherits all of the `compose.mk` standard library and primitives, it has the same native support for things like [workflows]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/demos/workflows), [containers]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/demos/matrioshka) and [polyglots]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/demos/polyglots).  But it also gets first-class [modules]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/modules) and a simple-but-powerful [metaprogramming environment]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/dialects).  

Since CMK uses `compose.mk` as the *interpreter*, it also [extends make]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/signals), [generalizes it]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/language/#homoiconic), and adds new kinds of [patterns]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/matrioshka).

Writing code in the CMK language does not add new core capabilities to what `compose.mk` already does *without* CMK.  But by *elevating existing idioms to native syntax*, the payoff is actually huge, because notation is part of how we think.[^1]

#### Running Demos 
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

The `cmk` subcommand is the public interface for CMK programs (`run` | `compile` | `build` | `doc`):

```bash {.cli_example}
# Run a demo directly, via its shebang (`#!/usr/bin/env -S ./compose.mk cmk run`)
$ ./demos/cmk/example.cmk

# Run a demo explicitly (`cmk <file>` is shorthand for `cmk run <file>`)
$ ./compose.mk cmk run ./demos/cmk/example.cmk

# Compile only, without running (highlighted preview to a terminal)
$ ./compose.mk cmk compile ./demos/cmk/example.cmk
```
That preview still requires an external `compose.mk` to access library functions.  But a stand-alone version with an *embedded* standard library is one step away: pass an output path, and `cmk compile` emits a self-contained, directly-runnable program:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk cmk compile ./demos/cmk/example.cmk example
$ chmod +x ./example && ./example
```

#### Compilation vs Transpilation 
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

For all the nerds out there, yes, CMK -> Makefile is really *transpiling*.. a source-to-source transformation.  Thus "interpretation", which piggy-backs on `make`, is really transpilation + execution, and doesn't involve anything like bytecode and VM.

If you were expecting "compilation" to create an executable artifact, that's more like "packaging".  See instead the docs for [self-extracting executables]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/demos/packaging/#makeself-reference) and  [guests & forks]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/standard-lib/#forks-guests-and-payloads).

### Basic Example
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

Let's revisit the JSON emit/consume example that's part of the [structured IO docs]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/standard-lib/#structured-io-basics).  The original code was simple, but rewriting this in CMK is much more pretty:

{{site.embed_demo(
  'demos/cmk/structured-io.cmk',type='cmk',
    bottom_right=site.link_mirror(
      'demos/structured-io.mk',
      base=mkdocs.site_relative_url+'/standard-lib'),
  github=github)}}

We'll get to the odd arrow syntax at the end of this section, but start with most basic stuff first. 

**Running CMK programs:**
:  This example runs via the **`cmk` subcommand** (its shebang is `#!/usr/bin/env -S ./compose.mk cmk run`).  `cmk run` compiles CMK -> Makefile and then executes the result, which is what lets us extend the base syntax and use the more exotic stuff you see in the rest of the file.  As with other demos, this one is executable directly.  Unlike a plain-Makefile demo, it *can't* be run with `make -f ..`.

**Implied Include + Standard Library:**
:  When using the interpreter, a line like `include compose.mk` is always implied, and this brings with it full access to the [standard library]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/standard-lib).

**Function Calls Rewritten:**
:  Awkward stuff like `$(call log.target, ...)` is rewritten as a function call `cmk.log.target(..)` instead.  Similarly, you get cleaner versions of [compose.import statements]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/bridge/#other-import-statements), or any of the other [macros]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/macros).

**Recursive Calls Rewritten:**
:  Awkward stuff like `${make} foo` can be written as `this.foo` instead.  Recursive invocation of the current context is common, and absolutely critical for code-reuse.[^3]  The choice of `this` for a new keyword is actually configurable too.. see [user-defined dialects]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/dialects/#user-defined-extensions).

Finally, **about that arrow syntax?**  These operators help to make [structured IO]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/standard-lib/#structured-io) feel more native, and are sugar to safely use `jq` and `jb` for parsing and pushing JSON, respectively, *regardless* of whether those tools are available on the host.  (A hint about what's happening here: glancing at the [original code]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/standard-lib/#structured-io-basics) that's "ported" to CMK-lang here, you'll notice the only "code transformation" we really need to get from **CMK -> Makefile** in this case involves very simple string substititions.)

