### The `⇐` Assignment Operator
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">
<a name=assignment-operator></a>

The `⇐` operator captures a command's output into a shell variable, i.e. the ubiquitous ``x=`cmd` `` idiom without the backtick noise.  Simple enough, but the useful part is that the right-hand side runs through the dialect first.  So for example:

```Makefile  {.language-makefile}

# CMK Assignment:
x ⇐ this.t  

# Lowered to Makefile:
x=`${make} t`
```

The whole RHS is captured up to the next shell separator (one of `;`, `&&`, `||`), a trailing line-continuation, or end-of-line.  As usual, `⇐` is inert inside define-blocks.

### Triple-Quoted Forms
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">
<a name=triple-quote-literals></a>

CMK has 3 triple-quoted literal forms.  Each emits its own body as text (handy for feeding a snippet or whole program into a pipeline without the usual quoting/escaping hassle), each supports multi-line content, and each is **inert inside `define`-blocks** (so a python `'''docstring'''` inside a [polyglot](#polyglot-examples) is left untouched).  

They differ only in *what gets interpolated*.  One caveat applies to all three: the body can't contain an internal run of three-or-more of its own delimiter character.

#### Triple-Quote (Singles)
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

The form for `'''…'''` is literal (to the shell).  Internal quotes and `%` are taken literally.  However the usual two-layer expansion applies:

* **Make** variables and functions DO expand (`${VAR}`, `$(VAR)`, `$(call …)`)
* **Shell** constructs do NOT expand: a shell variable `$$VAR`, a command-substitution
  `` `cmd` ``, and `$$(…)` are passed through verbatim (the single quotes protect them).

```Makefile {.language-makefile}
X := MAKE_VALUE
demo:
  '''mk=${X} sh=$${USER}'''
  # output is mk=MAKE_VALUE sh=${USER} and the shell var stays literal
```

**Lowering:** The compiler lowers this form as a **single-quoted** `printf '%s' '…'`.

#### Triple-Quote (Backticks & Doubles)
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

The forms for `"""…"""` and ` ```…``` ` are both interpolating.  These two are **equivalent**; just use whichever form doesn't clash with the body.  On top of the make expansion above, the **shell** layer now interpolates as well: `$$VAR`, `` `cmd` ``, and `$$(…)` all expand at runtime.  (Single quote = literal, double quote = interpolating, just like in shell.)

```Makefile {.language-makefile}
X := MAKE_VALUE
demo:
  """mk=${X} sh=$${USER}"""
   # output is mk=MAKE_VALUE sh=<value of $USER>
```

**Lowering:**  Both of these triple-quote forms lower to a **double-quoted** `printf '%s' "…"`.  

### Call Forms
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">
<a name=callable-targets></a> <a name=call-forms></a> <a name=call-form></a>

Call-forms use two kinds of "call anchors", or no anchor at all.  No-anchor is exactly the Receiver Form documented [elsewhere](#receivers).  

#### About Call Anchors

There are two call anchors because CMK treats both *targets* and *macros* callable, but these cases need to be differentiated.  Anchors are **not** keywords, because their actual value can be determined by [the dialect](#claude-link).  By default though, the convention is:

* Targets anchor: **`this.<name>`**
* Macro anchor: **`cmk.<name>`**

Both anchors share a common grammar, which also comes in two flavors.

* Argument Passing: **`<anchor>.<name>(<args>)` **
* Stream Passing: **`<anchor>.<name>[<stream>]`**

#### Lowering & Quick Examples
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

```Makefile {.language-makefile}

demo:
  this.eval["""hello world"""]
  # printf "hello world" | ${make} eval

demo:
  this.eval'''hello world'''
  # the "tagged string" form: same, no brackets

demo:
  this.b[this.a]
  # chain: ${make} a | ${make} b

demo:
  cmk.render(name=app)["""$$PAYLOAD"""]   
  # printf "$${PAYLOAD}" | $(call render,name=app)
```

#### Callable Properties
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

The last example above shows that call forms can be combined, and expressed in either order, so that `anchor.name(..)[..]` is the same as `anchor.name[..](..)`.  Since this is unambiguous to the parser it's supported as a matter of convenience, but in actual fact *argument-passing* comes first.

Argument passing is straight-forward.  A macro/function call-form supports comma-separated pos-args or keyword-arguments in the same way as the underlying function, whereas target call-form requires pos-args.

Streams are more interesting.

* Stream forms can be nested / composed to any depth, left-to-right.
* Streams can be [triple-quote form](#triple-quote-literals) 
* Streams support Via interpolating triple-quote form, streams support multi-line commands.
* Streams can be a single-line command verbatim
* Streams can be a [block-reference](#block-references), 

### Receiver Form
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">
<a name=receivers></a>

The primary [call-form](#call-form) anchors are explicit.  A **receiver** is a more implicit third option: a name brought into scope by a [declaration]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/macros/#declare-namespace).

This form covers various first-class constructs like a [channel](#claude-link), a [module](#claude-link), a [polyglot](#claude-link), or a [container](#claude-link).   After declaration, the name is implied, but otherwise the standard call-form grammar applies.

#### Lowering & Quick Example
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

```Makefile {.language-makefile}
# creates a named stack, registers `inbox` as a receiver
declare.channel(namespace=inbox)

# -> printf "type=login user=alice" | ${make} inbox.emit
inbox.emit["""type=login user=alice"""]

# logins=`${make} inbox.filter.field_equal/type,login`
logins ⇐ inbox.filter.field_equal/type,login
```

Since the declaration builds targets dynamically, the 2nd form above is just a small change over the target-calling form we've already met: `this.inbox.emit[...]`

!!! tip "OOP?"
    This resembles message-passing OOP since the *receiver* is the "object" a message is sent to and `receiver.method[...]` deliberately reads that way.  But it's simpler than that.  There are no instances, no inheritance, and no `self`.  
    
    A receiver is a compile-time **namespace**, not a runtime object, and the send is resolved by static name-rewriting rather than dynamic dispatch.  Any state involved lives *outside* the "object" (in this case a channel's queue is a stack-file, not a field of `inbox`).  Think *namespaced message-send syntax* rather than classes.. this gives the first-class look-and-feel without a real object model.

A receiver-send fires **only** when all three hold, which is what keeps it from meddling with ordinary names:

* The name was **declared** (an undeclared `foo.bar[...]` is left untouched),
* It sits at a **word boundary** (the start of a recipe word, not mid-token), and
* It is followed by a **call-suffix** -- `(`*args*`)`, `[`*stream*`]`, `/`*arg*, or an adjacent triple-quote.

So a target *definition* (`inbox.seed:`), a parametric def (`inbox.scan/%:`), a bare prerequisite
(`__main__: inbox.seed`), and anything inside a `define`-block are all left as-is. A legacy
`this.inbox.emit[...]` still works and is never double-anchored.

Receiver names are discovered from the file's own declarations by naming convention -- `namespace=`
(channels, modules, imported defs/targets), `def=` (polyglots, single-image
containers), and the `⟦`/`🞹`/`⫻`/`⦕ as` block-sugar. Multi-service compose files are the exception:
their service names are only known at runtime, so they keep the explicit `<svc> ᐉ <target>` dispatch.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/events.cmk', github=github)}}

### Block References 
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">
<a name=block-references></a>
<a name=block-refs></a><a name=block-ref></a>
<a name=blockrefs></a> <a name=blockref></a>

A `define`-block keeps a snippet (a script, a program, a query) in one named place; a
**block-ref glyph** then feeds that block straight into a command, with no temp-file dance.

| Glyph | Reference | Becomes |
| :-:   | --- | --- |
| `⬦` *NAME* | a transient **stream FD** | process substitution  via `<(…)` |
| `⬥` *NAME* | a real **local file** | a tmpfile path, materialized on disk |

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/blockrefs.cmk', github=github)}}

Reach for `⬦` (hollow = ephemeral) when a tool can support a stream, because it saves you on disk IO.  Reach for `⬥` (filled = on disk) when it requires a real path.  As usual, both are inert inside `define`-blocks.

!!! note "Tempfile Lifecycles"
    The two glyphs have very different lifecycles.  The `⬦` form lowers to a process-substitution (`<(…)`): the shell owns the transient FD and tears it down automatically when the command exits, so there's nothing on disk to manage.  The `⬥` form lowers to a command-substitution that runs `mktemp` *every time the recipe line executes*, writes the block to that file, and substitutes the path into your command.  A new file is created per evaluation (so re-running a target, or referencing the same block twice, yields distinct paths).  These files land in a run-scoped scratch location (a `.tmp.cmk.brf.*` name keyed on the run id) and are **auto-removed at the end of the run** by the supervisor, so you don't have to manage them.  The manual `mk.clean` target is available as a backstop if a run is hard-killed before teardown.

### Recipe-Body Joining
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">
<a name=recipe-body-joining></a>

The newline-separated lines of a recipe body are joined into a single shell with ` && ` connectors *by default*.  A body written one-statement-per-line therefore shares state (variables, `cd`) across its lines and stops on the first failure. The joining is conservative and should not surprise recipes that already manage their own control-flow: a line that already ends in a connector (`&&`, `||`, `|`) just continues to the next, a line you split yourself with a trailing `\` stays one command, and `-`- or `+`-prefixed lines stand alone.

The ` && ` default is a *policy*, not a law: it is overridable per-file with the [`recipe_join` compiler pragma](#compiler-pragmas) below.

### Compiler Pragmas
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">
<a name=compiler-pragmas></a>

[Dialects]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/dialects/#about-dialects) and [sugar]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/dialects/#about-sugar) let a file extend the *syntax* it's written in.  A **pragma** is the third member of that family: a leading-comment JSON hint that sets *per-program knobs* in-source, in lieu of piling environment variables onto the shebang:

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/pragma.cmk', github=github)}}

Each key is normalized (lowercase convention; `.`/`-` fold to `_`) and exposed as a `CMK_PRAGMA_<NAME>` variable -- a namespace the compiler *alone* writes, so a pragma can never clobber an internal control variable.  Knobs resolve with precedence **pragma > environment > default**: a **scalar** *replaces* (the program's pragma wins over an invoker's env, with a warning), while an **array** *accumulates* like `+=` (e.g. `at_exit_targets` runs *in addition to* the framework's own cleanup).  Because a pragma sets make-time values, it cannot reconfigure the early supervisor/bootloader knobs (`CMK_SUPERVISOR`, `CMK_DISABLE_HOOKS`, ...) -- those stay on the shebang/environment.

The original knob, **`recipe_join`**, selects the [recipe-body joining](#recipe-body-joining) connector:

| value      | effect                                                                 |
| ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `&&`       | *(default)* fail-fast; the body shares one shell and stops on first error |
| `;`        | run-all; the body still shares one shell but does not stop on failure  |
| `none`     | no join -- each line becomes its own recipe-line (classic `make`), with **no shared shell state** |

!!! warning "`recipe_join: none`"
    With `none` there is no shared shell across recipe lines, so anything that relies on it stops working: `cd` between lines, the [`⇐` operator](#assignment-operator), and the `&&`-chained decorator forms.  Use it only when you specifically want classic per-line `make` semantics.

#### Lifecycle / boot knobs

A program's **before** and **after** hooks are a symmetric pair of list knobs: `bootloaders` run *before* make (sourced in the supervisor's shell, so they can register an `EXIT` trap or set up early state), and `at_exit_targets` run *after* (as make targets).  A bootloader is an inline named `define` -- pure bash, single `$` (it is `source`d, not run by make), distinct from a make target.  Two scalar toggles round it out: `hooks` (`off` disables target-rewriting) and `bootloader_disabled`.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/pragma-boot.cmk', github=github)}}

These boot knobs only take effect via `cmk run` (the path that owns both the source and the program's re-exec), and they are threaded into the canonical `CMK_DISABLE_HOOKS`/`CMK_BOOTLOADER*` env -- *not* the `CMK_PRAGMA_*` namespace -- because the bash header reads them before make exists.  The scalars are pragma-wins with a loud notice; the `bootloaders` list *appends* to any invoker-set `CMK_BOOTLOADER`.

!!! warning "`bootloaders` source code"
    A bootloader is sourced as shell at boot -- powerful, and the same surface the shebang already exposes, but less visible.  An invoker can always block it with `CMK_BOOTLOADER_DISABLED=1`.  The **supervisor** itself (`CMK_SUPERVISOR`) is the one boot knob a pragma can never set -- it must come up before anything can be read.

#### Execution mode: `repl`

Where `recipe_join` declares how a program *compiles* and the boot knobs declare how it *boots*, the **`repl`** pragma declares how it *runs*: a program's **default action** becomes an interactive REPL over its own target namespace, rather than a single pass of `__main__`.  Set `repl: true` and `cmk run <file>` launches the harness; at launch it lists the program's own (local) target namespace, and typing a target name (one of those, or anything in the core namespace) runs it -- `python -i` / `irb`, for a `.cmk`.  The eval loop is the generic core dispatcher `mk.repl.kernel`, so this needs **no** import, `host_only`, or launcher in the program -- just the pragma:

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/repl.cmk', github=github)}}

The object form `repl: { read: <t>, eval: <t>, print: <t>, exit_after: 1 }` wires the harness's three regions (a scrolling **print** stream, the **eval** input sink, and a one-line **read** mode-line) to the program's own targets -- the reflective overlay in `demos/cmk/overlay.cmk` is just this.  An explicit `cmk run <file> <target>` **bypasses** the mode and runs that target (like `python script.py` vs bare `python`), and `cmk repl <file>` **forces** the mode on *any* program -- even one with no pragma -- as a universal interactive shell over its targets.  Like the boot knobs, this only applies via `cmk run` / `cmk repl` (the runtime owns the harness); the harness itself is the `tux.repl` plugin, built on first use.

This completes the **dialect + sugar + pragma** triad.  A natural next step (not yet implemented) is bundling all three under one named language, selectable in a single line -- the way Racket's `#lang` names a whole language -- so a project-specific DSL can be reused across files without repeating its hints.

### Indentation
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">
<a name=indentation></a>

Plain Makefiles require recipe lines to begin with a literal **tab**.  CMK relaxes this to **python-style indentation**: a target body may be indented with **tabs or spaces**, and the compiler normalizes a consistently space-indented body to the tab Make needs.

The rules, like Python's, are about *consistency*:

* A body indented with **tabs** is passed through unchanged (so you keep Make's "extra tab = literal command content" behavior).
* A body indented with **spaces** must use the *same* indent on every line; it is rewritten to a single leading tab.
* It is an **error** to mix tabs and spaces *within one indent* ("mixed mode"), or to use inconsistent amounts of space-indentation in one body ("mismatched").  The compiler stops with a clear message, much like Python's `TabError` / `IndentationError`.

Indentation is normalized as the **last** preprocessing step, after [sugar
blocks]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/dialects/#about-sugar) have been lowered.  See the {{macros.repo_link('demos/cmk/space-indented.cmk', name='space-indented demo', github=github)}}.

### Status Info, Limitations, & Other Advice
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

The CMK compiler is largely *vibe-coded awk*, but the test suite is massive, and the specifications, language-semantics, compiler-stages, and the general design is carefully crafted by a real human.  [Please report bugs]({{github.repo_url}}/issues), but not until you've **[confirmed you're using GNU awk!]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/quickstart/#compatibility-notes)**.

Everything that's documented here is stable and unlikely to change.  Per the rest of the design philosophy, the goal is to [freeze CMK after it's **useful** and **capable of being extended**]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/but-why#forever-development-considered-harmful) and most future changes are probably optimizations rather than extensions.

[make it the user-defined default]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/dialects/#user-defined-dialects). 
